    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 16px;
    display: flex;
    gap: 12px;
  }}
  .takeaway-icon {{
    font-size: 20px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-top: 2px;
  }}
  .takeaway h4 {{
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: #fff;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }}
  .takeaway p {{ font-size: 12px; margin-bottom: 0; }}
  .footer {{
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 24px 60px;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 11px;
  }}
  .two-col {{
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 20px;
  }}
  .highlight-box {{
    background: rgba(232, 119, 34, 0.08);
    border: 1px solid rgba(232, 119, 34, 0.3);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 14px 18px;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }}
  .highlight-box p {{ color: var(--text); margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 13px; }}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="header">
  <div style="max-width:1200px;margin:0 auto;">
    <div class="header-top">
      <div style="display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:center;">
        <span class="ticker-badge">NKE</span>
        <span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:13px;">NYSE &bull; Nike Inc.</span>
      </div>
      <span class="report-date">Historical Analysis &bull; January 2026</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Nike Inc. (NKE) Historical Stock<br>Performance Analysis</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">26-Year Comprehensive Review &bull; January 2000 – January 2026 &bull; Investor-Grade Research</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="container">

  <!-- KPI Dashboard -->
  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Cumulative Return</div>
      <div class="kpi-value" style="color:#4caf82">+1,282%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Jan 2000 → Jan 2026</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card blue">
      <div class="kpi-label">CAGR</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">10.60%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Annualized over 26.1 years</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card red">
      <div class="kpi-label">Max Drawdown</div>
      <div class="kpi-value" style="color:#e05c5c">−68.5%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Nov 2021 → Apr 2025</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card purple">
      <div class="kpi-label">Ann. Volatility</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">31.2%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Based on daily returns ×√252</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card green">
      <div class="kpi-label">Avg Daily Volume</div>
      <div class="kpi-value" style="font-size:22px">10.7M</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Shares per trading day</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Executive Summary -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-num">1</div>
      <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="highlight-box">
      <p><strong>Nike delivered a 1,282% cumulative return</strong> (10.6% CAGR) over the 26-year period from January 2000 to January 2026, turning a $10,000 investment into approximately $138,200 — a compelling long-term compounder despite significant cyclical volatility. The stock navigated multiple systemic crises and emerged stronger after each, until entering a pronounced structural decline from its all-time high of $166.25 in November 2021.</p>
    </div>
    <p>Nike's stock trajectory can be divided into three distinct eras: a volatile but resilient recovery phase through the 2000s (culminating in near 200% decade returns), a remarkable bull decade through the 2010s (+604%), and a mixed 2020s where pandemic-era euphoria gave way to inventory challenges, margin compression, and competitive headwinds driving the stock from its all-time high back toward decade lows.</p>
    <p>The stock's all-time high of <strong>$166.25</strong> was reached on November 5, 2021. As of January 30, 2026, NKE traded at <strong>$61.81</strong>, representing a 62.8% decline from peak. The maximum drawdown from that 2021 peak reached −68.5% by April 2025, underscoring that even elite compounders carry substantial downside risk over extended periods.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Price Trend Analysis -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-num">2</div>
      <h2>Long-Term Price Trend Analysis</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <div class="chart-title">NKE Monthly Closing Price (Adjusted) — January 2000 to January 2026</div>
      <canvas id="priceChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="phase-grid">
      <div class="phase-card">
        <h4>Phase 1: Early 2000s Trough &amp; Recovery (2000–2004)</h4>
        <p>NKE fell from ~$4.47 to an adjusted all-time low of <strong>$2.47</strong> by February 2000, amid the dot-com selloff and broader consumer discretionary weakness. The stock then staged a powerful recovery, gaining +163% from trough to end-2004 as Nike leveraged global brand expansion and supply chain improvements.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="phase-card">
        <h4 class="blue">Phase 2: Steady Growth &amp; GFC (2005–2009)</h4>
        <p>A multi-year grind higher was interrupted by the 2008 Global Financial Crisis, which knocked NKE from ~$12.60 to <strong>$10.16</strong> (−18% that year). However, Nike's defensive brand characteristics and international diversification limited losses relative to the broader market. The stock recovered fully by 2009.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="phase-card">
        <h4 class="green">Phase 3: Decade Bull Run (2010–2019)</h4>
        <p>Nike's most impressive sustained run: the stock rose from ~$13.27 to <strong>$93.45</strong>, a +604% decade return. Key drivers included the North American athleisure boom, DTC (Direct-to-Consumer) pivot, iconic product launches, and multiple consecutive years of double-digit earnings growth. 2013 (+53.8%) and 2019 (+38.2%) were standout years.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="phase-card">
        <h4 class="red">Phase 4: Pandemic Surge &amp; Structural Decline (2020–2026)</h4>
        <p>COVID-19 caused a brief but sharp 35% drawdown in March 2020 before e-commerce tailwinds and stimulus-fueled spending drove NKE to its all-time high of <strong>$166.25</strong> (Nov 2021). What followed was a severe multi-year decline: supply chain normalization, China headwinds, and strategic missteps drove the stock down 68.5% to $52.31 by April 2025 before a partial recovery to $61.81.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="annotation-row" style="margin-top:20px;">
      <div class="annotation">
        <div class="annotation-label">All-Time High</div>
        <div class="annotation-val">$166.25</div>
        <div class="annotation-desc">November 5, 2021</div>
      </div>
      <div class="annotation red">
        <div class="annotation-label">Adjusted All-Time Low</div>
        <div class="annotation-val">$2.47</div>
        <div class="annotation-desc">February 25, 2000</div>
      </div>
      <div class="annotation blue">
        <div class="annotation-label">Current Price</div>
        <div class="annotation-val">$61.81</div>
        <div class="annotation-desc">January 30, 2026</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Volatility Analysis -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-num">3</div>
      <h2>Volatility and Risk Assessment</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="chart-container">
        <div class="chart-title">Rolling 63-Day Annualized Volatility (%)</div>
        <canvas id="volChart"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-container">
        <div class="chart-title">Maximum Drawdown From Running Peak (%)</div>
        <canvas id="ddChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>Nike's annualized volatility averaged <strong>31.2%</strong> across the full 26-year period — meaningfully above the ~15–20% typical of large-cap defensives, reflecting the consumer discretionary nature of the business. Three distinct volatility regimes are visible:</p>

    <div class="annotation-row">
      <div class="annotation">
        <div class="annotation-label">Early 2000s Crisis</div>
        <div class="annotation-val">~65–80% Peak Vol</div>
        <div class="annotation-desc">Feb–Mar 2000: NKE-specific selloff triggered extreme intraday swings as institutional holders rotated out of consumer discretionary into tech alternatives.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="annotation blue">
        <div class="annotation-label">GFC (2008–2009)</div>
        <div class="annotation-val">~50–60% Peak Vol</div>
        <div class="annotation-desc">Systemic credit crisis elevated cross-asset correlations. Nike's relative stability limited but did not eliminate volatility spikes during the Lehman collapse period.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="annotation red">
        <div class="annotation-label">COVID Crash (2020)</div>
        <div class="annotation-val">~55–70% Peak Vol</div>
        <div class="annotation-desc">March 2020 saw NKE lose 35% in 5 weeks as global store closures threatened near-term revenue. Volatility normalized rapidly on DTC acceleration tailwinds.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Volume Analysis -->
  <div class="section">
